Encryption: three real choices, one honest refusal, and the command for each
==========================================================================

The setup screen offered "whole disk / archive / nothing" and explained none of them. Now it offers
three specific things and a fourth that is just the truth:

  * **LUKS — whole disk (recommended).** Protects everything on the disk, including files not yet
    created. Decided before the system is installed, or by reinstalling. Strongest, most disruptive to
    adopt later.
  * **age — one key file, encrypted palace.** The palace becomes a single file you can move or back
    up; one key file, modern defaults, no ceremony.
  * **GPG — a key you already have.** Most compatible, fiddliest; unattended decryption puts the
    secret on the box.
  * **Nothing.** With the warning shown only when it applies: anyone who gets this disk, or any
    account on this machine, reads every conversation in it. Reasonable on a desktop in a locked room,
    a bad trade on a laptop.

Each choice shows the actual command — `tar czf - palace/ | age -p -o palace.tar.gz.age`, and the GPG
equivalent — because **this app does not encrypt anything itself**. A menu that implies otherwise is
worse than the warning it replaced. Running those commands is the next piece of work, not this one.

